[% set v = c.values_ref %]
[% set h = c.helper %]
{{- /*
===============================================================================
Secret of the "[[ c.name ]]" component
===============================================================================
File generated by forge.

forge NEVER writes a secret value: neither in the specification, nor in the
values, nor in this manifest. This template only produces a *placeholder* — the
list of the keys [[ c.name ]] expects, with empty values. The real values are set
outside the repository: "--set", vault, secrets operator.

Two uses, decided by "secret.create":
  create: true  — the chart creates this Secret from the declared keys;
  create: false — this file produces nothing, and the chart references the Secret
                  named by "secret.existingSecret", managed outside the chart.
                  That name MUST then be filled in: the helper would otherwise
                  fall back on a derived name that does not exist, and the pods
                  would stay stuck waiting for the Secret. This constraint is
                  documented here rather than enforced by "required":
                  "required" would make "helm template" fail, and therefore the
                  validation of the chart and the golden tests, including when
                  nobody is deploying anything at all.

Only "stringData" is emitted, never "data". Both coexist in the API — "data"
expects base64, "stringData" plain text — but a key present in both blocks sees
"data" win, silently and without warning. Emitting only one of the two removes
the trap.

The type is free: "Opaque" suits arbitrary keys. A specialised type imposes its
own (kubernetes.io/tls requires exactly tls.crt and tls.key,
kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson requires .dockerconfigjson) — in that case, the
declared keys must be those ones, and those only.

No value is hardcoded here: everything comes from the "[[ c.name ]].secret" key
of values.yaml.
===============================================================================
*/}}
{{- if and [[ v ]].enabled [[ v ]].secret.enabled [[ v ]].secret.create }}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: {{ include "[[ h ]].secretName" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "[[ h ]].labels" . | nindent 4 }}
{{- /*
  Quoted: "kubernetes.io/tls" thus stays a string, whatever the YAML parser that
  reads the manifest back.
*/}}
type: {{ [[ v ]].secret.type | quote }}
{{- /*
  As for the ConfigMap, the block is only rendered if it has content: a
  "stringData:" followed by nothing amounts to null, and null is refused where the
  schema expects a table of strings. With no declared key, "{}" is therefore
  written.

  Keys and values go through "quote": stringData only accepts strings, and a value
  left empty must come out as "" and not as null. That is precisely what this
  template produces as long as nobody has filled in a value — the placeholder
  exists, it is empty, and nothing sensitive is versioned.
*/}}
{{- with [[ v ]].secret.stringData }}
stringData:
  {{- range $key, $value := . }}
  {{ $key | quote }}: {{ $value | quote }}
  {{- end }}
{{- else }}
stringData: {}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
